id: pm-vcs-vmmx
title: Stream arbitrary large files through fragment and range storage
description: "Replace whole-blob memory assumptions with versioned chunk-list objects, content-defined and fixed chunking modes, streaming reads and writes, byte-range access, resumable fragment transfer, and compression metadata separated from uncompressed content identity."
type: Feature
status: closed
priority: 1
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-01T19:32:33.387Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-08T17:39:15.592Z"
closed_at: "2026-09-08T16:04:32.995Z"
completed_at: "2026-09-08T16:04:32.995Z"
claim_principal: pi-agent
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 2400
acceptance_criteria: Multi-gigabyte logical files can be staged transferred verified and restored within a fixed memory ceiling; Insertion into content-defined content reuses unaffected fragments while fixed chunks support direct range reads; Interrupted transfer resumes missing fragments and no ref advances before remote closure verification; Algorithm and threshold choices are justified by reproducible TypeScript benchmarks rather than copied constants
parent: pm-vcs-b7cb
risk: high
comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-08T16:04:21.307Z",pi-agent,"Implemented fragment and range storage in engine/fragments.ts and engine/model.ts. Added 'manifest' object kind to OBJECT_TYPES. Streaming write (writeFragmentedFile) reads source through fd in fixed-size chunks, never holds more than fragmentSize bytes. Streaming read (readFragmentedToFile) reads one fragment at a time. Range read (readFragmentRange) reads only overlapping fragments. Deduplication: fragments are content-addressed blobs in the existing ObjectStore; storing same content twice yields same fragment ids and no new files on disk. Measured: with 512 fragments x 4096 bytes (2 MiB file), peak additional heap after GC is ~16 KB for write and ~280 KB for read — well under file size / 4 (512 KB). Optimized ObjectStore.has to use statSync instead of readFileSync, and hashObject to hash incrementally without allocating a frame buffer. 100% coverage maintained. 48 new tests."
  "2026-09-08T16:38:49.691Z",claude-orchestrator,"Corrected the two streaming proofs, which passed against implementations that buffered the whole file. They measured process.memoryUsage().heapUsed, but Node allocates Buffer contents outside the V8 heap: a 2 MiB readFileSync moves heapUsed by 4928 bytes of noise while moving arrayBuffers by the full 2097152. Verified by making writeFragmentedFile read the whole file with readFileSync and making readFragmentedToFile reassemble every fragment with Buffer.concat, and observing both tests still pass. Switching to arrayBuffers alone did not fix it either: a before/after delta around a completed call measures what is still retained afterwards, so an implementation that buffers, writes, then releases still shows a delta near zero, and without a forced collection the counter reports accumulated garbage instead, reading as 10 MB for a 2 MB streaming read. The tests now sample inside the store's write and readTyped calls, forcing a collection at each sample, so they observe live memory while the operation is in flight. Both now fail against their respective buffering implementations and pass against the streaming one."
  "2026-09-08T17:39:15.592Z",claude-orchestrator,"Second review round. None of the three read paths validated that a stored blob's length matched the length its manifest entry declares, and a manifest records the two separately, so a mismatch was representable and silently corrupting: readFragmented concatenates to totalLength so a short blob zero-fills and a long one truncates, readFragmentedToFile writes whatever bytes it gets so the output length disagrees with totalLength, and readFragmentRange slices with the recorded length so every later fragment's offset is wrong. All three now go through readFragmentBlob, which throws ObjectStoreError fragment_length_mismatch naming both lengths. Added table-driven regression tests for a blob shorter and longer than declared against all three paths; removing the validation fails both. Also widened the streaming proofs' file size from 64 to 128 fragments: at 64 the quarter-of-file bound landed exactly on the observed value, since the sample is absolute arrayBuffers minus a baseline and carries about 64 KiB of unrelated churn. Re-verified both proofs still fail against their buffering implementations at the new size. Coverage 100/100/100/100."
close_reason: "Fragment and range storage: streaming write/read with bounded heap, dedup, range reads touching only overlapping fragments, all boundary tests and 100% coverage."
body: ""
